  "slug": "colliculus-superior-99d6",
  "summary": "Repeated and two-year fMRI designs now test whether network connectivity and vascular markers reliably track effort, control, and reward-related behavior.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these four preprints is the relationship between measured brain network properties and individual differences in effort, cognitive control, and value-based behavior. One study tests whether effort-related behavior and nucleus accumbens resting connectivity show psychometric reliability across repeated sessions in 84 adults. Another relates regional arterial elasticity in cortical networks, estimated with pulse-DOT PReFx, to cognitive control costs during task switching across brain networks. A third shows that working-memory load in a dynamic bandit task reduces directed exploration and reconfigures aIns-IPS coupling within an fMRI decision network. A two-year longitudinal fMRI study of 332 emerging adults links rising ventral attention network connectivity to reward and punishment sensitivity, with self-control decline as the mediating pathway. The new element is the emphasis on longitudinal and repeated-measure designs, paired with network-level readouts of vascular and connectivity markers tied to behavior.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
